00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.180
This is the 3M N95 mask. It's ubiquitous by now.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.580
There's another type of mass called an N95, or respirator mask.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.599
What I want to show you guys is these N95 respirators.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.590
The N95 masks that 3M is so crucial to production of our in turn so crucial to our health care workers.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.110
It usually costs around $1.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.950
It's one of the most essential pieces of equipment to keep health care workers safe

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.329
while fighting the Corona virus

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.576
by filtering out 95% of all airborne particles --

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.100
Hence the name N95.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.960
It's also an extremely short supply.

00:00:36.679 --> 00:00:40.430
Well as the Corona virus continues to spread, the demand for N95 respiratory masks

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.590
around the world is also growing.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.499
The masks should be for the health care workers. The public should not be buying masks.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.210
But there are other masks besides the N95.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.000
This is the FFP2 to mask from Europe.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.149
It keeps out 94 percent of airborne particles.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.240
This is the K995 mask from Asia.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.270
It keeps out 95 percent of airborne particles.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:06.159
Yet the U.S. can not import any of these during the crisis.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.370
We 're really trying to dig into the exactly why this is happening on it on a daily basis,

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:18.599
but it looks, for now like the government simply did not have enough of these

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.840
things on hand to respond immediately.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.249
The federal government reportedly had 12 million masks in its stockpile.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:31.139
The U.S. will reportedly need 3.5 billion masks during this pandemic.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:37.260
There's a myriad of reasons why we have insufficient stockpiles.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:43.859
The role of government is to plan for crises, to stockpile masks so that if we ever have a pandemic,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.959
we have enough masks on hand.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.250
And it looks like the government didn't do its part of the job there.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:57.459
And we do have a federal agency and the CDC that has been systematically underfunded.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.879
We haven't received the right pieces that haven't invested in stockpiles over the years

00:02:03.569 --> 00:02:04.569
Because of the short supply

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:10.289
black markets have popped up overnight where prices for the N95 have more than quintupled.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.009
An investigation into hundreds of thousands of medical grade masks

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.198
that were just sold at auction.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.750
43-year-old Baruch Feldheim is accused of selling a thousand N95 masks

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.099
to a doctor at a 700 percent markup.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.479
If you go to Craigslist, you can find N95 masks going for hundreds of dollars.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:35.910
States are also all in a bidding war with one another to procure these masks at inflated prices.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.930
If you have the capacity to make these products,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.479
we will purchase them and we will pay a premium.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.389
But the federal government says that they are not at fault for the shortage.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.481
The notion of the federal stockpile, was it to be our stockpile?

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.939
It's not supposed to be state stockpiles that they then use.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.389
So we're encouraging the states to make sure that they're assessing the needs.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.740
They're getting the data from their local, local situations

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.169
and then trying to fill it with the supplies that we've given them.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.719
So what the government stockpile not being the answer.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:10.649
Other avenues needed to be explored to fulfill the spike in demand.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.010
Just one problem.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.330
We have our own Food and Drug Administration.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.289
We have our own regulatory bodies.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.760
We have our own standards.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:22.199
And we tend to gravitate towards importing things that we know

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.399
and that we're comfortable with.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.280
And that's where the N95 mask comes in.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.468
But also, remember, this isn't just stockpiles from a federal stockpile,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.689
our point of view, but also the broader supply chain.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:38.669
3M produces a large amount of respirator masks, but they aren't the only ones.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.379
There are 50 percent of face masks are produced in China, for example,

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.249
and there are big suppliers that exist globaly.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:50.460
And remember those alternative masks we were talking about in the beginning?

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.399
Country equivalent standards do not fly in America

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:58.979
because they're not approved by the FDA or the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.469
despite being equivalent masks.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:06.779
But these standards are also and this is something we're seeing here again in this crisis

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:12.750
can be a barrier to trade and can be a barrier to getting supplies

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.659
into the right into right places.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:27.449
The masks that are currently produced in China are right now not approved for export or import in the United States

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:28.980
as a class one medical device,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.980
which puts a restriction on the amount of supply.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:37.470
So without a sufficient stockpile and options limited overseas, America has to look inward.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:44.220
The federal government is calling on the private sector to help fill the need of medical supplies.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:49.350
Honeywell will be the latest company to pivot and prioritize public health

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.220
by producing N95 masks at its facility here in Phenix.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.089
New Balance says many of its U.S. factories, including one in Lawrence,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.970
are now producing face masks for hospitals.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.953
We're seeing automakers start to get into the business of making ventilators.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.610
We're seeing distillers started to make hand sanitizer.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:12.910
There's a huge ramping up of industrial America that is happening.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.790
But as we saw during the Second World War and in previous wars.

00:05:17.154 --> 00:05:21.209
But with the first shipment of masks from China landing in New York in early April

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:27.029
and Trump enacting the Defense Production Act to manufacture more masks and respirators domestically,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.430
millions of masks are being made available.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:30.600
But it's not enough.

00:05:30.767 --> 00:05:35.670
The solutions that we need and one of the key lessons that I hope we also get out of this

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.180
in order to be able to diversify our supply chains,

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:39.858
to make sure that we've got sufficient inventory,

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.350
it requires us to take a global view and then a global piece

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:49.439
so that we can move product and move support from one region that is being impacted today

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:50.759
to another that's going forward.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:57.179
We're again at one of those moments where when you respond to a global crisis like COVID- 19,

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:05.689
the answer is taking care of things at home, but also of learning from your neighbors.

